{"kind":"request","text":"Correct the Work view's attention semantics and Board presentation. A satisfied commitment may carry a stale provenance qualifier, but completed work must not be labeled or grouped as “Needs attention” solely for that reason. Keep lifecycle status primary, distinguish historical staleness from actionable responsibility, and preserve Kanban columns when attention-related filtering is used.","body":{"branch":"request/ui-attention-semantics","conditions":"A satisfied commitment never displays the contradictory pair “done” and “ATTENTION” solely because its chain is stale; completed stale or superseded history is described as historical/stale rather than actionable and is placed after current work; “Needs attention” is used only when a reader can identify a meaningful unresolved condition or next action; “Needs my action” remains the distinct personal-responsibility view; Board view retains lifecycle columns and attention-related filtering does not flatten mixed statuses into one card grid; disputed and lane-less exceptional states receive an honest, bounded presentation; counts and labels agree with these semantics; the four observed topic-alias cards #2679, #786, #2670, and #2659 are regression cases; UI tests cover status/qualifier combinations, filtering, counts, and board placement; the embedded UI is rebuilt; relevant UI and Go gates pass; an independent agent approves the exact implementation head before merge.","priority":"usability-bug","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}